module co-supreme-court-civil.certiorari

import co-state-rules.{ DecisionFiled, MandateIssued, ReviewPetitionFiled }

// Review on certiorari of a court of appeals civil judgment, from the
// petitioner's side. Every period below is stated in a note, not counted:
// computation is C.A.R. 26(a), with the legal holidays of C.A.R. 26(b),
// for which lit/core has no counting rule. The docket fee (C.A.R. 51(b))
// is set by statute, which this package does not state. The outline named
// no leading decisions; the one kept is Menefee v. City & County of
// Denver, 190 Colo. 163, 544 P.2d 382 (1976), as the compilation's
// annotation to C.A.R. 54 reports it.
start petition

decision petition: "Petition for writ of certiorari?" by applicant cite "Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 52(b)"
{
  note "A petition for writ of certiorari is filed within 42 days after entry of the final judgment on appeal, whether by opinion or by order, if no petition for rehearing is filed, or within 28 days after the intermediate appellate court denies rehearing or issues a new or modified opinion after granting it; no certiorari proceeding may be initiated until the time for rehearing in the intermediate court has expired; a timely petition, or a timely motion to extend the time for one, transfers jurisdiction to the supreme court (Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 52(b)). In workers' compensation and unemployment insurance cases the petition is filed within 28 days after the court of appeals opinion if no rehearing petition is filed, or within 14 days after rehearing is denied (C.A.R. 52(b)(2)). After appearance and payment of the docket fee the supreme court may for good cause extend the time or permit a late petition (C.A.R. 56). The petition complies with C.A.R. 32 and contains, in order, tables of contents and authorities, an advisory listing of the issues, a reference to the reports of the opinion, the grounds of jurisdiction with the dates of the judgment and any rehearing order, any pending case granted on the same issue, a statement of the case, the argument for issuing the writ with preservation and standard of review, and an appendix with the opinion (C.A.R. 53(a)). A stay of the intermediate court's decision is first sought in that court (C.A.R. 55)."
  edge-petition-none "No petition: the court of appeals judgment stands" -> no-petition {
    authority cite "Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 52(b)"
  }
  edge-petition-filed "Petition filed" -> review-decided {
    event ReviewPetitionFiled()
    authority cite "Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 52(b)"
  }
}

decision review-decided: "Certiorari granted or denied" by court cite "C.A.R. 49"
{
  note "Review on certiorari is a matter of sound judicial discretion, granted only for special and important reasons, such as a question of substance decided in a way probably not in accord with the supreme court's decisions, a conflict between divisions of the court of appeals, or a departure from the accepted and usual course of judicial proceedings calling for the court's supervision (C.A.R. 49). A cross-petition is filed and served within 14 days after service of the petition (C.A.R. 53(b)); an opposition brief is not required, and a respondent's is filed and served within 14 days after service of the petition, a petitioner's within 14 days after service of a cross-petition (C.A.R. 53(c)). A petition, cross-petition, opposition brief and combined cross-petition and opposition brief contain no more than 3,800 words, a reply brief no more than 3,150; a motion to exceed the limit is filed with the document and granted rarely (C.A.R. 53(f))."
  edge-review-decided-denied "Denied" -> review-denied {
    authority cite "Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 54(b)"
  }
  edge-review-decided-granted "Granted" -> briefing { authority cite "C.A.R. 54(a)" }
}

state briefing: "Merits briefs" by applicant cite "C.A.R. 57"
{
  note "On a grant the clerk issues an order notifying the lower court and counsel, and the certified transcript of record on file is treated as though sent up in response to a formal writ (C.A.R. 54(a)). Merits briefs comply with the content and length requirements of C.A.R. 28 and the form and service requirements of C.A.R. 25 and 32, and are filed within the time C.A.R. 31 prescribes, except that in workers' compensation cases the petitioner serves and files its opening brief within 14 days and the respondent its brief within 7 days after service of it, and no other brief is permitted; briefs filed below may not be incorporated by reference (C.A.R. 57)."
  edge-briefing-argue "Briefs closed" -> argument { authority cite "C.A.R. 34(a)" }
}

state argument: "Oral argument or submission" by court cite "C.A.R. 34(a)"
{
  note "Oral argument is at the court's discretion; a request for oral argument is filed as a separate document no later than 7 days after briefs are closed (C.A.R. 34(a))."
  edge-argument-decide "Decision" -> decision { authority cite "C.A.R. 35(a)" }
}

state decision: "Decision on the writ" by court cite "C.A.R. 35(a)"
{
  note "The court may dismiss or affirm, vacate, modify, reverse or set aside the judgment and remand; it issues a written opinion when vacating, modifying, reversing, setting aside or remanding (C.A.R. 35(a)). The judgment is entered when the court issues or announces its dispositive order or opinion (C.A.R. 36). The court may instead dismiss the writ as improvidently granted (Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 54(b))."
  edge-decision-issued "Opinion or order entered" -> rehearing-decision {
    event DecisionFiled()
    authority cite "C.A.R. 36"
  }
}

decision rehearing-decision: "Rehearing sought?" by applicant cite "Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 40(c)"
{
  note "In certiorari proceedings a petition for rehearing may be filed after an opinion on the merits of a granted writ, or when the court, after granting the writ, dismisses it as improvidently granted; never after an order denying the petition (Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 40(c)(3)). Unless the time is shortened or extended by order it is filed within 14 days after entry of judgment and states with particularity each point overlooked or misapprehended (C.A.R. 40(a))."
  edge-rehearing-decision-none "No petition" -> mandate {
    authority cite "Rule Change 2024(11), C.A.R. 41(b)"
  }
  edge-rehearing-decision-sought "Petition filed" -> rehearing-order {
    authority cite "Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 40(c)"
  }
}

state rehearing-order: "Rehearing decided" by court cite "C.A.R. 40(a)"
{
  note "If the petition is granted the court may make a final disposition without reargument, restore the case for reargument or resubmission, or issue any other order (C.A.R. 40(a))."
  edge-rehearing-order-mandate "Rehearing decided" -> mandate {
    authority cite "Rule Change 2024(11), C.A.R. 41(b)"
  }
}

state mandate: "Mandate issued" by office cite "Rule Change 2024(11), C.A.R. 41(b)"
{
  note "Unless the court grants or removes a stay or otherwise changes the time, the supreme court mandate issues no earlier than 14 days after entry of the judgment, or immediately where a petition for rehearing is not permitted; if rehearing is denied, or an extended time for it runs without a petition, no earlier than 2 days after the order denying it or the extended deadline; and immediately when a United States Supreme Court order denying certiorari is filed (Rule Change 2024(11), C.A.R. 41(b)(2))."
  edge-mandate-final "Mandate issued" -> decided {
    event MandateIssued()
    authority cite "Rule Change 2024(11), C.A.R. 41(b)"
  }
}

terminal no-petition: "No petition: the court of appeals judgment stands" outcome [final] cite "C.A.R. 41(b)"
{
  note "The court of appeals mandate issues under C.A.R. 41(b)(1): lit/co-state-appellate."
}

terminal review-denied: "Certiorari denied" outcome [final, loss] cite "Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 54(b)"
{
  note "No mandate issues on a denial; the clerk issues an order and notifies the lower court and counsel; no petition for rehearing may be filed after it, and jurisdiction returns immediately to the intermediate appellate court (Rule Change 2026(05), C.A.R. 54(b)). The compilation's annotation to C.A.R. 54 reports that a denial is addressed to sound judicial discretion and is not a determination of the issues on the merits (Menefee v. City & County of Denver, 190 Colo. 163, 544 P.2d 382 (1976)). Federal certiorari (28 U.S.C. § 1257) belongs to lit/scotus."
}

terminal decided: "Decided on the writ; mandate issued" outcome [final] cite "Rule Change 2024(11), C.A.R. 41(b)"
{
  note "The case returns to the court below with the mandate. Federal certiorari (28 U.S.C. § 1257) belongs to lit/scotus."
}
